Q: How can I check a JSP form request submit in the same JSP page? (enctype=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;) A: <p>When using <code>multipart/form-data</code>, the request is being sent in a different encoding than the standard <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> encoding which the <code>getParameter()</code> and consorts are relying on. That's why they all return <code>null</code>.</p>
<p>You need to parse the <code>multipart/form-data</code> request into sensible objects before being able to determine the submitted values. For that, normally the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Commons" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apache Commons</a> <a href="https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-fileupload/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">FileUpload</a> or <code>request.getParts()</code> is being used. For further details, check <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2422468/how-to-upload-files-in-jsp-servlet/2424824#2424824">this answer</a>.</p>
<p>Alternatively, you can also just check the request method. A normal request (link, bookmark, etc.) is always <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Transfer_Protocol#Request_methods" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GET</a>. A <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTML_form" rel="nofollow noreferrer">form</a> submit to upload the file is always <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POST_%28HTTP%29" rel="nofollow noreferrer">POST</a>.</p>
<pre><code>if (&quot;POST&quot;.equalsIgnoreCase(request.getMethod())) {
    // The form was submitted.
} else {
    // It was likely a GET request.
}
</code></pre>
<p>But this doesn't make any utter sense inside a JSP file. Writing raw Java code down in a JSP file is a recipe for maintenance trouble. Rather submit the form to a fullworthy <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/tags/servlets/info">servlet</a> and just implement the <code>doPost()</code> method. In a servlet you've all the freedom to write Java code without taking all that HTML content into account.</p>
